script: [
{ say: 'Part 2. You will hear a guide welcoming visitors to a science museum. First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 15.' }, { pause: 4 },
{ s: 'A', t: "Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the Harcourt Science Centre. My name is Imogen, and I'll be your guide for the next hour. Before we set off, I'd like to go through a few practical points, because I've found that a little planning makes a visit much more enjoyable." },
{ s: 'A', t: "The Centre was founded about forty years ago by a group of local teachers who wanted children to see science as something exciting, rather than something found only in textbooks. That idea is still at the heart of everything we do, and you'll notice that almost every exhibit invites you to touch it, turn it or press it. Please do. Nothing here is meant to be admired from a distance." },
{ s: 'A', t: "First, the opening days. Some of you may remember that the museum used to close on Tuesdays. That changed in the spring, and we are now closed on Mondays instead, so please bear that in mind if you're planning to come back with friends or family. On every other day we're open from nine thirty until five." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Next, the crowds. Our busiest period is between ten and twelve, when most of the school groups arrive. They're very lively, and some of the hands-on exhibits can have long queues. If you have a choice, I'd recommend coming after two o'clock, when it's much calmer and you can spend as long as you like at each exhibit. The last entry is at four thirty." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Now, coats and bags. You'll see rows of lockers just inside the entrance. Each locker needs a one-pound coin to lock it, but you get the coin back when you unlock it, so you don't actually lose any money. If you've only got notes, the reception desk will give you change." },
{ s: 'A', t: "If you are using a wheelchair or pushing a buggy, you'll find that all four floors can be reached by lift, and there are accessible toilets on every level. We also have a limited number of folding stools that you can borrow from reception if you'd like to sit down while a presenter is talking. Just ask any member of staff in a green jacket." },
{ s: 'A', t: "If you feel hungry or thirsty at any point, there's a café. It used to be on the ground floor next to the shop, but since our new Space Room opened last year it has moved to the top floor, and it has wonderful views across the river. The food is simple, but the soup is excellent." },
{ s: 'A', t: "You're very welcome to take photographs for your own use. All I ask is that you switch off the flash, because it can damage some of our older exhibits and it spoils the displays for other visitors. Filming with a camera on a stand is not allowed without permission." },
{ s: 'A', t: "One last thing before we begin. If you'd like an audio guide, you can collect one from the information desk. They're available in six languages and cost three pounds. They're optional, of course, because I'll be explaining the main points as we go." },
{ s: 'A', t: "The museum shop is next to the exit, so you won't need to carry anything round with you. It sells books, kits and a very good range of science toys for all ages, and a small part of every sale goes towards our schools programme, which brings classes from rural areas to the Centre free of charge." },
{ pause: 1.5 },
{ say: g.LTI[6] }, { pause: 3 },
{ s: 'A', t: "Right, let's look at what each gallery offers. We'll start on the ground floor with the Energy Hall, which is the big room on your left. Every day at twelve and again at three o'clock, one of our presenters gives a live demonstration, including a machine that makes real lightning. It's loud, so do warn any young children beforehand." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Opposite the Energy Hall is the Ocean Lab. This is where you can try our new virtual reality headsets. You put one on, and you feel as if you're sinking slowly down to the seabed, with fish and jellyfish drifting past you. It's very popular, and it takes about ten minutes per person." },
{ s: 'A', t: "The lab was built with help from a team of marine scientists, and everything you see on screen is based on real survey data collected from research ships. If you enjoy it, there is a short film about their work at the far end of the room." },
{ s: 'A', t: "If we go up the stairs to the first floor, we reach the Body Zone. This one is particularly aimed at families. There are free workshops for children under ten every day, where they can listen to a heartbeat or build a model of a lung. Parents are welcome to join in." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Next to it is the Space Room, which has a planetarium dome. Because the dome only seats forty people, you need to book a time slot at the desk by the door. If you want to see the show this afternoon, I'd suggest doing that straight after this talk, because the places do fill up." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Inside, the show lasts about twenty-five minutes. It takes you from the surface of the Earth out past the planets, and the commentary is aimed at anyone from eight to eighty, so please don't feel it is only for children." },
{ s: 'A', t: "And finally, at the end of the corridor, is the Inventors' Workshop. This is a favourite of mine. Visitors use the materials we provide to build a small machine of their own, such as a simple robot or a windmill, and you're allowed to take whatever you make home with you. There's no extra charge." },
{ s: 'A', t: "There's also a quiet room beside the lift if you need a break from the noise, but that's not part of any gallery. Now, if you'd all like to follow me, we'll start with the Energy Hall. Please stay together, and do ask me anything you like as we walk." },
],
