id: 't16p2', test: 16, part: 2, title: 'A welcome to the farmers market', titleVi: 'Giới thiệu chợ nông sản địa phương cho cư dân mới', topic: 'Local community', level: 'B1+',
context: 'The manager of a local farmers market explains how the market works to people who have recently moved to the town.',
speakers: { A: { name: 'Ingrid (market manager)', accent: 'en-GB', gender: 'f' } },
script: [
{ say: 'Part 2. You will hear the manager of a farmers market talking to people who have recently moved to the town. First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 15.' }, { pause: 4 },
{ s: 'A', t: "Good morning, everyone, and welcome to this short orientation session for new residents. I'm Ingrid Haldane, and I manage the Eastbrook Farmers Market, which many of you will have noticed on your way here. I'd like to explain how it works, so that you can get the most out of it from your very first visit." },
{ s: 'A', t: "First, a little history. The market began twelve years ago with just eight stalls in the old square beside the church. It grew quickly, and by last year we had forty producers asking for a pitch, but the square could only hold fifteen of them. Nobody ever complained about flooding, and the council had no plans to rebuild the square. We simply ran out of room. So two years ago we moved to the car park behind the library, which can take up to sixty stalls." },
{ s: 'A', t: "The market is open every Saturday from eight until one, and on the first Sunday of each month we hold a special market with craft stalls as well as food. People often ask me who is allowed to sell. You might imagine that every trader must be a farmer, but that isn't so. We welcome bakers, cheesemakers and people who make jam, as long as everything they sell is grown or made within thirty miles of the town. Anything brought in from further away, such as imported fruit, is not allowed, and we check this with a visit to every producer once a year." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Now, a tip about timing. If you want the best choice, come before nine. By ten, the most popular bread and the freshest vegetables have often gone. You may be tempted to wait until the end, because some traders reduce their prices in the last half hour, but the choice is poor by then, and I wouldn't recommend it unless you are very flexible about what you buy. Around eleven it gets extremely crowded, with long queues at the coffee stall, so that is the worst time for a quiet visit with a pushchair." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Payment is the next thing to explain. Cash is welcome, and a few stalls take cards, but the mobile signal in the car park is weak, and the card machines often fail. So we introduced market tokens. You can buy them at the information tent by the entrance, and every stall accepts them. Each token is worth one pound, and you can exchange any that you don't spend before closing time, so there is no risk." },
{ s: 'A', t: "I'm often asked what I am proudest of. The number of visitors has doubled since the move, and we now welcome around three thousand people each Saturday. We also keep our prices among the lowest in the region. But what I'm most proud of is our school gardening club. Last spring about thirty children grew vegetables in raised beds beside the market and sold them on a stall of their own, and they raised enough money to buy tools for their school. For me, that is what a market should do." },
{ pause: 1.5 },
{ say: g.LTI[6] }, { pause: 3 },
{ s: 'A', t: "Now let me introduce five of the stalls that you will find, in the order that you meet them as you walk in from the entrance. The first is Hillside Bakery, on your left. Their sourdough is excellent, but it is baked in small batches, and it sells out before ten almost every week. If you want a loaf, please don't arrive late." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Next is Mossgate Dairy, which makes cheese from its own herd of goats and cows. They always have a table of small pieces for you to try, and nobody pushes you to buy anything, so it's a good place to learn what you like." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Third, there is the honey stall run by the Bramble family. They don't offer tastings, for hygiene reasons, but you can order larger jars in advance and collect them the next Saturday. That's very useful before Christmas, when they often run out of stock." },
{ s: 'A', t: "The fourth stall sells fish. The fresh fish comes from the coast, which is almost eighty miles away, so the van can make the trip only once a month, on the first Saturday. If you see it, buy it, and if you miss it, you will have to wait." },
{ s: 'A', t: "The last is Old Orchard Preserves, at the far end of the car park. It's a jam and chutney stall that changed hands in the spring, when the original owners retired and a young couple took over. They kept the old recipes but have added some new flavours, so it's worth another look if you tried it years ago." },
{ s: 'A', t: "A few practical points before I finish. Please bring your own bags, because we no longer provide plastic ones, and many traders will give you a small discount if you bring back a clean egg box or jam jar. Dogs are welcome as long as they are kept on a lead, but the cheese and fish stalls ask that they are not brought close to the counter." },
{ s: 'A', t: "If you would like to help, we are always looking for volunteers to put up the stalls at seven in the morning, and to collect the leftover vegetables at closing time. That food goes to the local food bank, and nothing is wasted. You can sign up at the information tent, or you can write your name on the list by the door today." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Please also take a look at the map on the wall behind me, which shows where each stall stands, and the leaflet beside it, which lists every producer, what they sell and which days they attend. Finally, you can find a weekly newsletter on our noticeboard and at the library, telling you which producers are coming and what is in season. Thank you for listening, and I hope to see you all on Saturday." },
],
