speakers: { A: { name: 'Felix (radio presenter)', accent: 'en-GB', gender: 'm' } },
script: [
{ say: 'Part 2. You will hear a local radio presenter talking about changes to household recycling collections. First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 15.' }, { pause: 4 },
{ s: 'A', t: "Good morning, and welcome to the Ravenbrook local news. If you live in the district, you may have noticed a leaflet from the council on your doormat this week. It explains some big changes to the way your recycling is collected, and I've been finding out what they will mean for you." },
{ s: 'A', t: "First, the timing. The changes were originally planned for the beginning of January, but the new collection lorries arrived late, so the council has moved everything back. The new system will start on the first Monday in March. That is not next week, as some listeners have been asking me, but a little over a month from now, so there is plenty of time to get ready." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Now, why is the council doing this at all? Some residents have suggested that people asked for fewer collections, and others have said it is because of a new national law. Neither is correct. Government targets for recycling do exist, but the law does not require this particular change. The real reason is that around one load in five delivered to the sorting plant was being rejected, because it was dirty or contained the wrong kind of plastic, or had food waste mixed in. Rejected loads are sent to landfill, and that costs the council a great deal of money." },
{ s: 'A', t: "So, what will actually change? Let's start with plastic bottles, food tins and cans. The council first proposed collecting these every two weeks. However, more than four thousand residents replied to the consultation, and the great majority said that every two weeks would not be often enough, particularly for large families. The council listened, and plastic and cans will continue to be collected every week." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Paper and card, on the other hand, will move to every two weeks, because the volumes are much more predictable. And glass will no longer go into the main bin at all. Instead, every household will receive a small green box for bottles and jars, which will be emptied once every four weeks. You will not need to apply for one. They will be delivered to your door during February." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Next, the garden waste service. At the moment the brown bins are collected all year round, but the figures show that in the winter months most of them are almost empty. So from March, garden waste will be collected only between March and November, with no collections in December, January or February. People who pay for the service will get a refund for the winter months, and the council says it will be applied automatically." },
{ s: 'A', t: "What about the occasions when a collection is missed? Many people tell me that the council phone line is always busy, and the good news is that you will no longer need to use it. The phone line for missed bins is closing. Instead, you should report it on the council's website within two days of the missed collection. If you leave your bin out and do nothing, the crew will not return automatically, so do remember to report it." },
{ pause: 1.5 },
{ say: g.LTI[6] }, { pause: 3 },
{ s: 'A', t: "Finally, a number of streets will have a different collection day, and it is important to check yours. I'll go through the areas which are changing and, for those which are not, I'll let you know." },
{ s: 'A', t: "The Brookside Estate, which has always been collected on a Monday, will move to Tuesday. Marsh Lane was previously collected on a Wednesday, but it will take over the Monday slot that Brookside is leaving. Residents in Kingsway, who are used to a Tuesday collection, will move to Thursday. The Orchards, on the other hand, will have their bins emptied on a Wednesday from March, a change from their old Friday collection." },
{ s: 'A', t: "The only area in this list that stays exactly as it is, is the Old Town, which remains on a Friday. I have also had questions about weekends. There will be no household collections on a Saturday or a Sunday under the new system. The Saturday collections in the town centre are for shops and businesses only, and they are not affected." },
{ s: 'A', t: "A few other details to finish. Bulky items such as old sofas or fridges can still be collected, at a charge of twenty-two pounds for up to three items, and you need to book that online as well. The council will also run drop-in sessions at the library on the first two Saturdays of February, where staff will be happy to answer questions and show you which items go in which container. If you would like the leaflet in a larger print size, or in another language, that is available on request." },
{ s: 'A', t: "One more practical point. The council is also introducing a free text message reminder service. You register your postcode on the website, and the evening before each collection you will receive a short message telling you which bins to put out. It is especially useful during the first few weeks, when everyone is getting used to the new timetable, and it can be switched off at any time if you find it annoying." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Residents who would find it difficult to carry their bins to the kerb, for example because of an injury or a disability, can ask for an assisted collection, so that the crew will collect the bins from beside the house. That is a free service, but it has to be arranged in advance, and the council has asked me to say that the form is available at all the libraries as well as online." },
{ s: 'A', t: "That is all the recycling news for now. After the break, we'll hear from the owner of a bakery in Old Town whose cakes have won a national prize, and then it is on to the weather." },
],
