script: [
{ say: 'Part 3. You will hear two literature students, Imogen and Kofi, discussing an essay plan with their tutor, Dr Alder. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24.' }, { pause: 4 },
{ s: 'C', t: "Right, let's make a start. Your joint outline on unreliable narrators is on my desk, and I have a few questions. Imogen, why don't you begin by telling me what led you to The Glass Orchard as your main text?" },
{ s: 'A', t: "Well, I'd read two other novels by Mairead Tolland, and I liked them, but that wasn't really the reason. What drew me to this book is that the story the narrator tells keeps shifting. Ruth Ames describes the same afternoon three times, and each version is a little different. I wanted to find out why a writer would do that." },
{ s: 'C', t: "That is a good starting point, because it gives you a real question rather than just a topic. Now Kofi, you drafted the title. I have a worry about it." },
{ s: 'B', t: "Is it too long? I did wonder." },
{ s: 'C', t: "Length is not the issue. The title says that you will compare how three novels create unreliable narrators, but you never say what you mean by the word unreliable. Readers use it very loosely. A narrator might lie deliberately, or be confused, or simply see things from a limited viewpoint, and those are quite different things." },
{ s: 'A', t: "So we need a definition at the start?" },
{ s: 'C', t: "A working definition, yes, within the first page, so that your argument has something solid to stand on. And with three novels you will only have room for brief summaries, so I would cut it back to two." },
{ s: 'B', t: "I agree with that, actually. I was getting nervous about the word limit." },
{ s: 'C', t: "Which second novel would you keep? Choose one whose narrator contrasts with Ruth, so that the comparison is sharp and gives you plenty to say." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Probably the book by Idris Vane, where the narrator is a young detective who is convinced she is always right." },
{ s: 'C', t: "That would work well. Her confidence is a kind of unreliability too, since she ignores any evidence that does not suit her theory, and readers trust her at first precisely because she sounds so sure of herself." },
{ s: 'B', t: "I hadn't thought of certainty as a warning sign. That's a fascinating point, and it gives us a real contrast to build the whole argument on." },
{ s: 'C', t: "Tell me what you think is going on with Ruth. Is she deceiving the reader?" },
{ s: 'A', t: "I think she is. She hides things on purpose, because she is ashamed of what she did in the house." },
{ s: 'B', t: "I'm not so sure. To me it seems more likely that her memory is failing. She is eighty years old when she tells the story, and she even says that she cannot trust her own recollections. Surely that makes her honest rather than deceitful." },
{ s: 'C', t: "That disagreement could be the heart of your essay, so don't hide it. Argue it out openly, with evidence from the text for each view." },
{ s: 'A', t: "We did find an article by a critic called Hal Norwood that supports my reading. He says the author intends us to distrust Ruth from the first page." },
{ s: 'C', t: "I know the article. I would be careful with it. He writes with great confidence, but when you look closely he is mostly guessing what Tolland meant, and he offers very little proof from the novel itself. I would use it as an opinion to discuss, not as authority." },
{ pause: 1.5 },
{ say: 'Now look at questions 25 and 26.' }, { pause: 3 },
{ s: 'B', t: "We've also been trying to list the ways the novel makes readers doubt Ruth. At first we thought the dates in her account did not match." },
{ s: 'C', t: "I checked that, and the chronology is perfectly consistent, so you can drop it. The author does use two other devices, though, and they are worth explaining carefully." },
{ s: 'A', t: "One is that other characters give a different version of events. The gardener, for instance, says he was never in the house that afternoon." },
{ s: 'C', t: "Exactly. And the second?" },
{ s: 'B', t: "The letters. Ruth quotes from letters she says she received, but when the letters appear in full a few chapters later, they say something rather different." },
{ s: 'C', t: "Yes, that is the most striking example. Some critics also point to the shifts of tense, but I think that is a distraction. Do not make too much of it. There are no editor's notes in this edition, so you cannot use those either." },
{ pause: 1.5 },
{ say: g.LTI[9] }, { pause: 3 },
{ s: 'C', t: "Let's go through the plan section by section. Your introduction first. It runs to nearly four hundred words, and most of that is the plot of the book." },
{ s: 'A', t: "I thought the examiners would need to know the story." },
{ s: 'C', t: "They already know it. Give them two sentences of context and spend the rest of the space on your argument. At the moment it reads like a book report." },
{ s: 'B', t: "OK. The next section is on the narrative voice, where we look at how Ruth talks to the reader." },
{ s: 'C', t: "That section is the strongest part. You have chosen quotations that really show her habit of speaking directly to the reader and then taking things back. Keep it just as it is." },
{ s: 'A', t: "Thank you. The third section is about the ending, where she finally admits that the first version was false." },
{ s: 'C', t: "There you make a big claim, that the ending proves she was lying from the start. But you give one example only, and it is a short quotation. One more passage from the final chapter would make the case much stronger." },
{ s: 'B', t: "I can add the scene with the broken clock. That works." },
{ s: 'C', t: "Good. And your conclusion simply repeats what you have already said. What you need there is a clear answer to the question you asked at the beginning, which is why a writer would choose such a narrator in the first place." },
{ s: 'A', t: "So we say what the effect on the reader is, not just what the author does." },
{ s: 'C', t: "Precisely. Make the reader's experience your last point." },
{ s: 'B', t: "How many sources do you expect us to use?" },
{ s: 'C', t: "Five or six reliable ones is plenty. Quality matters more than quantity. And please check every page reference at the end, because that is where students lose marks for no good reason." },
{ s: 'B', t: "Should we send the plan by email, or bring a printed copy?" },
{ s: 'C', t: "Email is fine, and please put your names and the module code at the top of the document, because I mark a great many of these." },
{ s: 'A', t: "We will send you the revised plan on Thursday." },
{ s: 'C', t: "Good. Then we can fix the deadline for the first draft when we meet next week." },
],
